<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=galen">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">129–c. 216 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galen was a Greek physician of the second century CE whose writings dominated medicine for centuries. His works were translated into Arabic during the translation movement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, preserving them and shaping medical science in the Islamic world and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galen.jpg" alt="Galen" width="254" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg Paul Busch (engraver) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galenus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The House of Wisdom (Bayt al-Hikma), traditionally associated with Harun al-Rashid and reaching its zenith under al-Ma'mun, sponsored the systematic translation of Greek, Persian, Indian and Syriac texts into Arabic, a project that preserved Aristotle, Euclid, <b>Galen</b> and Claudius Ptolemy for the medieval world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Hellenistic and Roman periods, Greek was the language of science and scholarship: Euclid’s Elements of geometry, the works of Archimedes, the medical writings of Hippocrates’ school and of <b>Galen</b>, the astronomy of Ptolemy, and the critical scholarship of the Library of Alexandria, which edited and preserved the classics.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almagest of Claudius Ptolemy, written in the 2nd century CE, gave astronomers a geometrical model of the planets, built of circles upon circles, that predicted their positions well, and the writings of <b>Galen</b> governed anatomy and medicine.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Eight generations of the Christian Bukhtishu family served the caliphs as physicians, the Christian Hunayn ibn Ishaq translated <b>Galen</b> into Arabic, and Christian secretaries and Jewish bankers served the government.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.12,27.18&amp;year=185" aria-label="Pergamon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5223 761 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Galen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5673 1031h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5673 1031h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.12,27.18&amp;year=185">What was here in 185 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
